// `Vec<T>` -- `ntime`'s one growable sequence (`BUILD.md` B-12, TM-005).
//
// THE SHAPE IS THE COMPILER'S `List<T>`; THE TYPE IS OURS, and B-10 is why: a
// library must not import a compiler's internals. There is no prelude `Vec<T>`
// to import in any case -- `SAFETY.md` S-17b measured that, and the prelude's
// `List<T>` is now compiler-known and OWNING (the compiler's D-247), which this
// type is not. So none of the prelude's element handling applies here and all
// of it is this file's job.
//
// ===========================================================================
// THE RESTRICTION, WHICH IS THE FIRST THING TO READ (TM-132, TM-136, TM-150)
// ===========================================================================
//
// `Vec<T>` IS FOR A NON-OWNING `T`. Every function below is correct at a `T`
// that owns nothing -- an integer, a float, an enum without a payload, a struct
// of those. At an OWNING `T` (a `string`, a `buffer`, another `Vec`) this type
// does not discharge its element-lifetime obligation. The restriction was
// written against a compiler defect at cycle 0.0.4; its REASON has changed
// twice since, and the restriction has not.
//
// AT CYCLE 0.0.5 (TM-136). O-N17 -- the defect this restriction was written
// against -- IS FIXED at pin `aaffb87`; all five of its reproduction cases
// compile, link and run (`../../tests/probe/defect/generic_element_move/`,
// TRANSCRIPT PART D). Two things found in the same hour kept the restriction:
//
//   1. THE ELEMENT-DROP LOOP AS THIS FILE WOULD SPELL IT IS REFUSED. One
//      hoisted `#wild_slice` binding with `move(s[i])` inside a loop is
//      `NITPICK-MOVE-001`: moving out of an element invalidates the binding
//      the element was reached THROUGH, and the next iteration reads it
//      again. It is refused at `T = int64` as well, so it is a move-tracker
//      rule and not an ownership one. Re-making the slice inside the loop
//      body compiles; so does `move(v.items[i])` through the bare pointer,
//      which TM-129 forbids here because it loses the bounds guard.
//      (Unchanged at compiler `c3bdae2`, measured again there at 0.1.0b.)
//
//   2. `NITPICK-TYPE-046` WAS NOT ENFORCED INSIDE A GENERIC BODY (O-N19).
//      `T:answer = s[i]` at an owning `T` -- a COPY of an owner -- was
//      accepted at exit 0, linked and ran, while the identical statement with
//      `string` written out was refused; `case4` of
//      `../../tests/probe/defect/generic_owning_copy/` read the allocator's
//      0xAA poison through the second owner and exited 170.
//
// AT CYCLE 0.1.0b (TM-150), THE SECOND ONE CLOSED. At compiler `c3bdae2`
// `NITPICK-TYPE-046` fires inside a generic body: the compiler's D-264 makes a
// bare type parameter move-only in the body that names it, because a generic
// body is checked ONCE for every type it is instantiated at, some of which own
// storage. The copy is refused where it is written -- at a scalar
// instantiation too -- and `generic_owning_copy/` now asserts the refusal,
// with `aaffb87`'s verdicts as its control. It refused this file's own
// `vec_reserve` loop, which is why that function now relocates with `ralloc`.
//
// THE SECOND ONE HAD ALREADY BITTEN THIS FILE. `vec_pop<T>` shipped at 0.0.4 as
// `T:answer = s[v.count - 1i64]` -- `case1`'s function exactly -- and the
// comment beside `vec_at` claimed such a copy was "refused by TYPE-046",
// which was false at every pin before `c3bdae2`. The `move` was written at
// 0.0.5, where it always belonged, and it is still the right spelling: a pop
// TRANSFERS ownership, and D-264 names `move(...)` as the way a `T` place is
// copied out.
//
// SO THE RESTRICTION NOW STANDS ON ONE REASON: FOUR OF THE NINE OPERATIONS
// BELOW OWE AN ELEMENT DROP AT AN OWNING `T` -- `vec_set`, `vec_clear`,
// `vec_truncate` and `vec_free` -- and none of them discharges it. At a
// non-owning `T` none of them needs to and all nine link and run. (This
// paragraph said FIVE until 0.1.0b and named `vec_pop` among them. `vec_pop`
// owes no drop: its `move` hands the element to the caller. Measured at
// `c3bdae2` at `T = string`: two million push-then-pop cycles end with
// `peak_live` 120 bytes, and the same cycles with `vec_clear` in place of the
// pop end with all 48 000 096 retained, both at exit 0 -- TM-150.) It is NOT
// approximated here: `SAFETY.md` S-18b puts element lifetime AT THE
// INSTANTIATION, and the sanctioned concrete spellings are measured and
// committed -- `probe06_generic_vec.npk`'s `free_names` and `probe12b`'s
// `vec_set_string`.
//
// AT CYCLE 0.1.3c (TM-193, TM-194, TM-196) THE TYPE SAYS TWO THINGS THIS HEADER
// USED TO SAY FOR IT. `Vec<T>` IS MOVE-ONLY -- a copy of one is
// `NITPICK-TYPE-046`, and the section above the struct says how -- and THE ONE
// VERB THAT HANDS AN ELEMENT BACK BY VALUE ASKS FOR A `T` THAT OWNS NOTHING:
// `vec_at<T: Pod>` at an owning `T` is `NITPICK-TYPE-017` at the call, where
// it used to move the element out (its own comment says how). The four drops
// above are unchanged, and nothing in the language refuses them: a bound on
// the TYPE, `struct:Vec<T: Pod>`, would, and it is not taken (TM-194 says
// why). And the pair TM-150 measured in a scratch directory is committed:
// `../../tests/unit/vec_churn_pop.npk` and `vec_churn_clear.npk` hold 120 and
// 48 000 096 to the runtime's own count on every run.
//
// WHY THAT IS NOT A WORKAROUND (W-11). No function here is bent around the
// bug; each is the one that would have been written anyway. `0.0.4.md` §2
// required each element-discarding entry to have *"an element-drop path for an
// owning `T` OR a stated restriction to non-owning ones"* -- written before the
// defect was known. The defect removed the first option. **What would have been
// a workaround** is a generic `vec_clear<T>` that quietly does not drop: it
// would leak at an owning `T`, PASS `exit 0` because D-151 cannot see a managed
// body (TM-106), and read later as a design choice nobody would question.
//
// `ntime` NEEDS NO OWNING `T`. `Layout`'s vector (`FORMAT_MODEL.md` §3) is a
// payload-free enum but for `Literal(uint16)`, and the zone tables hold OFFSETS
// into a name pool precisely so no row owns anything -- which
// `check_no_owning_fields` enforces rather than trusts.
//
// ===========================================================================
// THE BOUNDS GUARD (TM-129, `SAFETY.md` S-17c)
// ===========================================================================
//
// `items` is a `wild T->` -- A BARE POINTER, WHICH THIS LANGUAGE DOES NOT
// BOUNDS-CHECK (TM-108). `ExprIndexExpr` calls `emit_bounds_guard` on
// `TY_SLICE`, `TY_ARRAY` and `TY_SIMD` and NOT on `TY_POINTER`, so an
// out-of-range `v.items[i]` is A WRONG VALUE, NOT A CRASH.
//
// So every accessor here lays a length-carrying slice over the block and
// indexes THAT, and the check becomes the compiler's own:
//
//     T[]:s = #wild_slice<T>(v.items, v.count);
//     pass raw s[i].pod_copy();       // `vec_at`; until cycle 0.1.3c, `pass s[i];`
//
// ONE UNSIGNED COMPARE COVERS BOTH ENDS. `emit_bounds_guard` emits `icmp ult`
// -- unsigned -- and `index_as_i64` sign-extends first, so a negative index
// becomes a huge unsigned value and the same compare rejects it. A hand-written
// `i >= 0` beside it would be dead code (measured: `probe13c` traps at `i ==
// -1` with no such comparison anywhere in the accessor).
//
// THE SLICE IS LAID OVER `count` FOR READS AND WRITES OF LIVE ELEMENTS, AND
// OVER `cap` ONLY IN `vec_push`, whose valid index is `count` itself. A guard
// over `cap` would accept an index into allocated-but-dead space, which is the
// distinction `probe13b` exists to catch: it has room at `i == count` because
// `cap` is larger, and it still traps.
//
// AND IT COSTS A CONSUMER NOTHING: `NITPICK-REACH-003` bills a program using
// this accessor six identities and bills the bare-pointer accessor THE SAME
// SIX. The guard makes the `OutOfBounds` arm honest; it does not add it.
//
// THE ONLY RAW `.items[` IN THE LIBRARY ARE IN THIS FILE, and
// `check_raw_index` fails the run on any other -- which is what keeps every
// index inside the accessor pair.
//
// ===========================================================================
// `items` IS HIDDEN, `count` AND `cap` ARE SEALED UNDER `ListLen`
// (cycle 0.1.0c, TM-156; the compiler's D-313, D-314, D-308)
// ===========================================================================
//
// SINCE CYCLE 0.1.0c THE PARAGRAPH ABOVE IS THE COMPILER'S RULE FOR EVERY
// OTHER MODULE, NOT OURS. `items` is `hidden`: code outside this module can
// neither read nor write it, so `v.items[i]` anywhere else is
// `NITPICK-TYPE-080` at compile time -- measured at `c3bdae2`, and pinned by
// `../../tests/probe/probe16b_vec_items_read_refused.npk`. What TM-108 and
// TM-129 kept true by convention, and `check_raw_index` by a tree check, no
// other module can now spell at all. `check_raw_index` stays: it still watches
// `Bytes`' `.ptr[`, which `sealed` does not hide (a read, and a write, through
// a sealed field's pointer both pass -- D-313; `bytes.npk` says what that
// leaves open), and it still watches THIS file's own bare-pointer bindings.
//
// `count` and `cap` are `sealed`: read anywhere, written only here, so a
// consumer's `v.count = 3i64` is `NITPICK-TYPE-079` (`probe16`). And each
// carries the prelude's `ListLen` -- `{ $ >= 0i64, $ <= 140737488355328i64 }`,
// the bound the compiler's own `List` and its built-in lengths use -- which
// is CHECKED AFTER EVERY WRITE, IN EVERY BUILD, trapping `LimitViolated`. So
// every consumer of this module owes that arm (109 in this ecosystem's
// failsafes, TM-152), and the check can only ever fire from inside this file,
// because nothing outside can write the field.
//
// THE PAYOFF IS THE VERIFIED BUILD'S, NOT A PLAIN BUILD'S. In a plain build
// the limit is one more check that correct code never trips. What it buys is
// that every READ of `count` or `cap` becomes a FACT the solver may assume
// (`VERIFICATION_REFERENCE.md` §2.1 at the pin: "every read is a fact") --
// which is what makes the bounds rows of the `#wild_slice` producer calls
// below, and `vec_reserve`'s `v.cap * 2i64` overflow row, dischargeable
// rather than opaque. That build is cycle 0.8's (`VERIFICATION.md` P-1b);
// nothing here claims it yet.
//
// WHAT HIDING AND SEALING DID NOT STOP, UNTIL CYCLE 0.1.3c: A WHOLE-STRUCT
// COPY. A `Vec<T>` was a plain struct over a `wild` pointer, so
// `Vec<int64>:w = v;` compiled and made a SECOND HANDLE on the same block, and
// `v = u;` overwrote all three fields at once from outside this module --
// neither names a field, so neither is a write the seal sees. Measured at
// `c3bdae2`: after the copy, `vec_free(@v)` and then `vec_at(@w, 0i64)` read
// the allocator's `0xAA` poison (exit 170) -- a use-after-free with no hidden
// or sealed field ever named. The workbench's question 9 carried it to the
// author beside `Bytes`' pointee, and the next section is the answer.
//
// ===========================================================================
// MOVE-ONLY BY CONSTRUCTION (cycle 0.1.3c, TM-193; `SAFETY.md` S-18g)
// ===========================================================================
//
// THE AUTHOR'S ANSWER TO QUESTION 9 (2026-09-25), AND `nitpick-regex`'S
// DESIGN, PORTED (its RX-161). The struct's last field is
// `hidden string[0]:move_only`, and each shape above is `NITPICK-TYPE-046`
// where it is written: a copy of a `Vec`, one `Vec` assigned over another, and
// a copy of a struct holding one (`../../tests/probe/probe16f_vec_copy_refused.npk`,
// `probe16g_vec_assign_refused.npk` and `probe16h_vec_holder_copy_refused.npk`;
// against the tree before, each compiles and reads the free poison). A transfer
// is `move(v)`, after which `v` is refused (`NITPICK-MOVE-001`), and a
// `move Vec<T>:v` parameter takes one; `../../tests/unit/vec_moves.npk` runs
// every shape the property still allows. It costs nothing measured: a `Vec`
// is 24 bytes, as before; no module's arm bill moves; and the drop the
// compiler now generates for a `Vec` walks the empty array and frees NOTHING,
// so the block is still `wild`, `vec_free` is still its release, and a `Vec`
// never freed still traps `WildLeak` at `exit 0` (96, measured).
//
// A BY-VALUE `Vec` PARAMETER IS A LOAN, AND A LOAN OF AN OWNER IS READ-ONLY.
// An ordinary parameter is one the callee may read and may not keep (the
// compiler's D-065 and D-183), so a `Vec` is passed to one without `move`, and
// every write through it -- `@v`, an assignment into it -- is
// `NITPICK-TYPE-085` (the compiler's DEF-102). That is why `vec_at`, the one
// function here that only reads, takes its `Vec` as a loan, and every function
// that changes one takes `Vec<T>->`.
mod:vec;

// The fields, in this order, matching `BUILD.md` B-12 exactly -- three until
// cycle 0.1.3c added `move_only` (TM-193).
// `Ord` derives in declaration order (the compiler's **D-123**, whose table
// reads "`Ord`, `PartialOrd` | lexicographic comparison in declaration order"),
// so field order is semantic even where nothing derives today.
//
// (This cited D-051 until cycle 0.0.6. At every pin this workbench has used,
// D-051 is "No `ostring`; portability lives in a `Path` type above `nlibc`" --
// a real heading, so nothing dangled and `check_specs_current` cannot see it
// by design: a compiler `D-nnn` is verified by READING it, which is what
// caught this. `SAFETY.md` §1 already cited the rule correctly, to
// `TRAITS_REFERENCE §2.5`, so the mis-citation was one site.)
//
// THE QUALIFIERS, SINCE CYCLE 0.1.0c (TM-156) -- see the header's last
// section for what each one does. ONE ORDER PARSES: `sealed limit<ListLen>
// int64:count`. Written the other way round, `limit<ListLen> sealed int64`, it
// is `NITPICK-PARSE-001` "expected a type" at the column of `sealed` --
// measured at `c3bdae2` -- although `TYPE_REFERENCE.md` §9.1.2's example at
// that pin shows the refused order; `VERIFICATION_REFERENCE.md` §2.1 shows the
// one that parses. `ListLen` needs no import: it is the prelude's `pub`
// `Rules<int64>` (D-308 §6), and it admits the VACANT value 0, which a field's
// rule must (`NITPICK-TYPE-077`). The fields are `int64` and not `T`, so the
// generic struct is no obstacle to `NITPICK-TYPE-059`. And a limited field has
// NO ADDRESS (`NITPICK-TYPE-063`): nothing here takes `@v.count` -- every
// function reaches the struct through a pointer and assigns the field, which is
// a write point, not an address.
//
// `move_only`, THE FOURTH FIELD (cycle 0.1.3c, TM-193): a fixed array of NO
// strings. It holds nothing and occupies nothing -- `#size_of<Vec<int64>>()`
// is 24, as it was -- and because its ELEMENT owns, the compiler's layout walk
// marks every `Vec<T>` as owning (its D-183), and an owning type is move-only.
// That is the whole mechanism; the header's last section says what it refuses.
// The same field over `int64[0]`, whose element owns nothing, changes nothing
// at all (measured): the element's ownership is what does it. `hidden`, so no
// module but this one can name it, and `vec_init` fills it with `[]`.
pub struct:Vec<T> = { hidden wild T->:items; sealed limit<ListLen> int64:count; sealed limit<ListLen> int64:cap; hidden string[0]:move_only; };

// `ensures answer.count == 0 && answer.cap >= 1`
//
// (`VERIFICATION.md` P-1b: an obligation is a comment in the syntax it would
// take unless a numbered decision accepts the arm its live clause costs every
// consumer. `answer` and not `result` -- `result` is a reserved
// `VerificationKeyword`, TM-130/B-18.)
//
// A REQUEST OF ZERO OR LESS BECOMES ONE, rather than an error or a null block.
// `alloc(0)` would give a block no element can be written to while `cap` said
// otherwise, and this type has no error identity to raise (`SAFETY.md` S-4:
// `core` declares none, and the budget is three with a ceiling).
pub func:vec_init<T> = Vec<T>(int64:cap) never fails {
    int64:n = cap;
    if (n < 1i64) { n = 1i64; }
    // `n * #size_of<T>()` is where this module's `IntOverflow` arm comes from,
    // and D-210 traps rather than wrapping -- so a caller asking for an absurd
    // capacity gets a trap and not a short block silently sized modulo 2^64.
    wild int8->:mem = alloc(n * #size_of<T>());
    pass Vec{ items: mem =>! wild T->, count: 0i64, cap: n, move_only: [] };
};

// `ensures v.cap >= v.count + need`
//
// GROWTH DOUBLES, so a run of pushes is amortised linear rather than quadratic.
// The new capacity is at least `count + need` and at least twice the old, which
// makes `vec_push`'s loop O(1) amortised without a special case for the first
// element.
pub func:vec_reserve<T> = NIL(Vec<T>->:v, int64:need) never fails {
    int64:want = v.count + need;
    if (v.cap >= want) { pass NIL; }
    int64:n = v.cap * 2i64;
    if (n < want) { n = want; }
    // THE RELOCATION IS ONE BITWISE MOVE OF THE WHOLE BLOCK, AND `ralloc` IS
    // THAT OPERATION (cycle 0.1.0b, TM-150). Until then this body allocated a
    // fresh block and copied element by element, `into[i] = from[i]`, and
    // `dalloc`ed the old one. At compiler `c3bdae2` that line is refused
    // `NITPICK-TYPE-046`: D-264 checks a generic body once for every type it
    // is instantiated at, so a copy of a `T` place would leave two owners at an
    // owning `T`. `ralloc` grows in place where it can, else allocates, copies
    // the old block's bytes and frees it -- the same bytes the loop moved, in
    // one call, with no element copy for D-264 to refuse and no loop to carry
    // a measure. So this row is correct at ANY `T` by construction, where the
    // loop was correct at an owning one by accident.
    //
    // `ralloc(p, 0)` traps and cannot happen here: past the early return,
    // `want > cap >= 0`, so `n >= want >= 1`. And a push after `vec_free`
    // still traps `Unreachable` (exit 95): the freed block now reaches the
    // allocator's membership check in `ralloc` rather than in `dalloc`,
    // measured at `c3bdae2`.
    wild int8->:mem = ralloc(v.items =>! wild int8->, n * #size_of<T>());
    v.items = mem =>! wild T->;
    v.cap = n;
    pass NIL;
};

// `ensures v.count == old(v.count) + 1`
//
// `vec_push` IS THE ONE ELEMENT-WRITING OPERATION THAT OWES NO DROP, and the
// reason must be stated because the line of code is identical to `vec_set`'s
// (TM-127, `SAFETY.md` S-18c): it writes AT `count`, which is past the last
// live element by construction, so there is nothing there to discard.
pub func:vec_push<T> = NIL(Vec<T>->:v, move T:x) never fails {
    drop vec_reserve(v, 1i64);
    // OVER `cap`, uniquely in this file: the valid index here is `count`
    // itself, which a slice over `count` would reject. `vec_reserve` above has
    // just made `cap > count`, so this index is in range by construction and
    // the guard is the belt.
    T[]:room = #wild_slice<T>(v.items, v.cap);
    room[v.count] = move(x);
    v.count = v.count + 1i64;
    pass NIL;
};

// ---- `Pod`: what `vec_at` may hand back by value (TM-194; `SAFETY.md` S-18h) --
//
// ONE METHOD, AND THE LANGUAGE DECIDES WHO MAY IMPLEMENT IT. `pod_copy` takes
// its `self` LENT and returns a `Self`, never failing. For a type that owns
// nothing, `pass self` is its copy. For an owning type the same body is
// `NITPICK-TYPE-047` -- a lent owner cannot be passed on (the compiler's
// D-065) -- so an impl the compiler admits for one would have to build an
// independent value, and a `never fails` body cannot allocate one. Measured at
// compiler `c970483`: `impl:string:Pod`, an impl for a struct holding a
// `string`, and `impl:Vec<int64>:Pod`, each written `pass self`, are each
// `TYPE-047` (`../../tests/probe/probe19_pod_owner_refused.npk` asserts the
// first). So `T: Pod` on `vec_at` states TM-132's restriction TO THE COMPILER
// for the one verb that hands an element back, at every consumer's call.
//
// THE TRAIT AND ITS NINE IMPLS ARE `nitpick-regex`'s, TEXT FOR TEXT, THE
// PARAMETER MODES INCLUDED (its RX-168); this library imports nothing
// (TM-027), so the trait is declared here rather than shared. The integers to
// 64 bits and `bool`: every scalar a specification here stores in a `Vec`. A
// type of a consumer's that owns nothing implements it in one line where the
// type is declared -- a struct of integers, or a payload enum such as
// `FORMAT_MODEL.md`'s parts -- and the umbrella re-exports the name so a
// consumer can (`../../tests/unit/vec_at_pod.npk`). A wider scalar is one line
// here.
//
// THE HOLE, SAID WHERE A READER MEETS IT: at compiler `c970483` an impl may
// declare its `self` `move` where the trait lends it, and the compiler accepts
// it -- the compiler's DEF-116, raised by `nitpick-regex`'s planning, whose
// fix (a parameter's `move` made part of the signature, refused
// `NITPICK-TYPE-014`) is at no pin of ours. Through `vec_at` such an impl hands
// back a second owner of the element: `impl:string:Pod` written with
// `move string:self`, read twice, frees one body twice, exit 95 on both legs
// (measured, `meta/roadmap/0.1/0.1.3c.md` §1.6). `TYPE-047`'s own message
// suggests exactly that `move` -- DO NOT TAKE IT HERE. No impl in this library
// does, and `nitpick-regex`'s `probe18_impl_adds_move.npk` pins the shape for
// the ecosystem.
//
// THE PRELUDE'S `Copy` WILL REPLACE THIS TRAIT (TM-198). On 2026-09-26 the
// author ratified the copy marker the compiler seat put to him, as its D-327:
// a prelude marker trait `Copy`, with impls for every copyable scalar and a
// derivable form for a struct of copyables, landing at the compiler's 1.6.1c,
// after this pin; `Copy` becomes a prelude-owned name, so nothing here is
// called that. At the re-pin that carries it the replacement is MECHANICAL AND
// CONFINED: this block -- the trait and its nine impls -- deleted;
// `vec_at<T: Pod>` bounded `T: Copy`, and its one `pod_copy()` call the copy
// D-327 spells; the umbrella's `Pod` line gone; and the three impls in
// `tests/` -- `vec_at_pod`'s two and `probe19`'s -- in `Copy`'s form. `Pod` is
// used nowhere else, on purpose.
pub trait:Pod = {
    func:pod_copy = Self(Self:self) never fails;
};
impl:int8:Pod = { func:pod_copy = int8(int8:self) never fails { pass self; }; };
impl:int16:Pod = { func:pod_copy = int16(int16:self) never fails { pass self; }; };
impl:int32:Pod = { func:pod_copy = int32(int32:self) never fails { pass self; }; };
impl:int64:Pod = { func:pod_copy = int64(int64:self) never fails { pass self; }; };
impl:uint8:Pod = { func:pod_copy = uint8(uint8:self) never fails { pass self; }; };
impl:uint16:Pod = { func:pod_copy = uint16(uint16:self) never fails { pass self; }; };
impl:uint32:Pod = { func:pod_copy = uint32(uint32:self) never fails { pass self; }; };
impl:uint64:Pod = { func:pod_copy = uint64(uint64:self) never fails { pass self; }; };
impl:bool:Pod = { func:pod_copy = bool(bool:self) never fails { pass self; }; };

// `requires i >= 0 && i < v.count`
//
// A READ, AND SINCE CYCLE 0.1.3c (TM-194) THE LANGUAGE HOLDS IT TO BEING ONE.
// It hands back a COPY of the element, through `Pod`'s `pod_copy`, which only a
// type that owns nothing can implement as declared (above), so at
// `T = string` the call is `NITPICK-TYPE-017`: *"`string` does not implement
// `Pod`, which `vec_at`'s parameter `T` requires"*. And it takes its `Vec` as
// a LOAN, the language's read-only parameter, where it took `Vec<T>->`: a
// `Vec` owns since the same cycle (the header's last section), and `@` of a
// lent owner is `NITPICK-TYPE-085`, so a pointer reader could not be handed a
// `Vec` its caller holds only on loan -- a by-value parameter, a field of a
// lent struct, another module's `sealed` field (`@` of one is
// `NITPICK-TYPE-079`). As a loan it reads all of them, and a local, as
// `vec_at(v, i)`, and through a pointer as `vec_at(<-p, i)`; and it can write
// none of them, which the compiler checks (`TYPE-085`). Measured,
// `meta/roadmap/0.1/0.1.3c.md` §1.5.
//
// THE GUARD IS UNCHANGED (TM-129, `SAFETY.md` S-17c): the index still goes
// through a slice laid over `count`, so an index out of range traps
// `OutOfBounds` at every `T`.
//
// UNTIL CYCLE 0.1.3c THIS WAS `vec_at<T> = T(Vec<T>->:v, int64:i)` ENDING
// `pass s[i];`, AND AT AN OWNING `T` IT REMOVED THE ELEMENT. `pass` of a PLACE
// moves implicitly, so at `T = string` the emitted body stored
// `zeroinitializer` over the slot and called `npk.vacant.<dty>` on it while
// leaving `count` untouched, and a second `vec_at` of the same index returned a
// length-0 string -- exit 11,
// `../../tests/probe/defect/generic_owning_copy/case5_vec_at_destructive.npk`,
// which asserts the refusal now. The language behaving as specified, and the
// reason the row was for a non-owning `T` (TM-136); the bound makes the reason
// the compiler's. (And until cycle 0.0.5 this comment said "at an owning `T` a
// copy is refused by TYPE-046", which was false in both halves.) A cycle that
// needs an owning element lifts TM-132's restriction by a decision, and the
// compiler's own `List<T>` is the shape it would take: no by-value get at all.
pub func:vec_at<T: Pod> = T(Vec<T>:v, int64:i) never fails {
    T[]:s = #wild_slice<T>(v.items, v.count);
    pass raw s[i].pod_copy();
};

// `requires i >= 0 && i < v.count`
//
// AT AN OWNING `T` THIS OWES AN ELEMENT DROP AND CANNOT DISCHARGE IT (TM-127,
// TM-132). Overwriting discards the element that was there, and a `wild T->`
// element does not drop for you -- `wild` is the manual regime. The correct
// spelling moves the outgoing element into a dying local first. That was
// O-N17's blocked primitive when this was written at 0.0.4; O-N17 is fixed,
// and written generically over this function's one slice it is refused
// `NITPICK-MOVE-001` instead -- the header's reason 1, measured again at
// compiler `c3bdae2` at cycle 0.1.0b. At a non-owning `T`, the type this file
// is for, there is nothing to drop and this is complete.
pub func:vec_set<T> = NIL(Vec<T>->:v, int64:i, move T:x) never fails {
    T[]:s = #wild_slice<T>(v.items, v.count);
    s[i] = move(x);
    pass NIL;
};

// `requires v.count > 0`
//
// Measured out of range: with `count == 0` the slice is empty and the index
// `-1` traps `OutOfBounds` rather than reading backwards off the block.
//
// THE `move` IS LOAD-BEARING AND WAS MISSING UNTIL 0.0.5 (TM-136). Without it
// this body reads `T:answer = s[v.count - 1i64]`, which at an owning `T` is a
// COPY of an owner: the returned value and the slot both hold the same
// pointer. `NITPICK-TYPE-046` exists to refuse exactly that and did not fire
// inside a generic body before compiler `c3bdae2`, so the row shipped at 0.0.4
// with the bug and a green suite beside it. AT `c3bdae2` IT DOES (D-264,
// TM-150): the body without the `move` is refused `NITPICK-TYPE-046` at
// compile time, whatever `T` it is instantiated at -- measured at 0.1.0b by
// deleting the word. A pop TRANSFERS ownership, so `move` is the spelling that
// was always correct; at a non-owning `T` it costs nothing, which is why
// writing it was never a workaround for the check that was missing then and
// is present now.
//
// AND IT IS OWNERSHIP-CORRECT AT AN OWNING `T`, which is why `vec_pop` is not
// among the header's four: the popped element leaves with its ownership and
// drops at the caller's binding. `../../tests/unit/vec_churn_pop.npk` holds two
// million push-then-pop cycles of `string`s to `peak_live <= 75000` -- it
// measures 120 -- where `vec_churn_clear.npk`, `vec_clear` in the pop's place,
// holds all 48 000 096 bytes (TM-196).
pub func:vec_pop<T> = T(Vec<T>->:v) never fails {
    T[]:s = #wild_slice<T>(v.items, v.count);
    T:answer = move(s[v.count - 1i64]);
    v.count = v.count - 1i64;
    pass answer;
};

// `ensures v.count == min(n, old(v.count))`
//
// At a non-owning `T` dropping the tail is exactly lowering `count`. At an
// owning `T` it would additionally owe a drop per discarded element (S-18c),
// which is the blocked path above.
pub func:vec_truncate<T> = NIL(Vec<T>->:v, int64:n) never fails {
    if (n < 0i64) { v.count = 0i64; pass NIL; }
    if (n < v.count) { v.count = n; }
    pass NIL;
};

// `ensures v.count == 0`
pub func:vec_clear<T> = NIL(Vec<T>->:v) never fails {
    v.count = 0i64;
    pass NIL;
};

// `ensures v.cap == 0 && v.count == 0`
//
// THE CONTRACT SAYS `cap == 0` AND NOT `items == NULL`, because this body does
// not null the pointer and a contract that claims more than the code does is
// the defect this repository amends specifications over. `0.0.4.md` §2's table
// wrote `v.items == NULL`; corrected here and in that table at cycle 0.0.4.
//
// AND THE LAST SENTENCE OF THAT PARAGRAPH WAS WRONG UNTIL CYCLE 0.0.6 (F1).
// It read "`cap == 0` is the ownership bit that matters, and it is what a
// later `vec_free` of the same value would read". THIS BODY READS NO FIELD
// BEFORE `dalloc`, so nothing here consults `cap`, and a second `vec_free`
// would `dalloc` the same pointer again whatever `cap` said. What actually
// contains a double free is the RUNTIME: measured at pin `aaffb87`, one free
// exits 0, and free-then-push-then-free exits **95** -- `Unreachable`, raised
// by the allocator and taken by `failsafe`. (Since `vec_reserve` relocates
// with `ralloc`, cycle 0.1.0b, it is the PUSH that traps -- `ralloc` meets the
// freed block first -- and the exit is still 95, measured at `c3bdae2`.) So
// `cap == 0` is a statement about the value's meaning to a CALLER -- an
// emptied `Vec` claims to hold nothing and to have room for nothing -- and it
// is not a guard. Stating a mechanism
// that is not the one doing the work is how a later reader removes the real
// one.
//
// EVERY SCOPE THAT MADE A `Vec` CALLS THIS, or `exit 0` traps under D-151 --
// which watches `wild` allocations, and this block is one. THAT GUARANTEE IS
// ABOUT THE BLOCK ALONE (TM-106, S-18b): D-151 cannot see a managed body, so an
// unemptied `Vec<string>` would pass `exit 0` and still leak. It is the reason
// this type is restricted to a non-owning `T` rather than trusted at any `T`.
// (Since cycle 0.1.3c a `Vec` owns, so the compiler generates a drop for one:
// it walks `move_only`'s empty array and frees nothing, and this is still the
// only release of the block -- a `Vec` never freed still exits 96, measured,
// TM-193.)
pub func:vec_free<T> = NIL(Vec<T>->:v) never fails {
    dalloc(v.items =>! wild int8->);
    v.count = 0i64;
    v.cap = 0i64;
    pass NIL;
};
